That's the bug. The fact {Englishman, Red} says "the house with the Englishman is red" — it does NOT say "a red house cannot have a Norwegian." The converse direction I implemented — "the house with Red must have the Englishman" — is actually valid in this puzzle (since there's exactly one red house and exactly one Englishman). Wait, is that so? Yes! There's exactly one red house and exactly one Englishman, and the Englishman lives in the red house. So the red house IS the Englishman's house. Therefore a red house cannot have a Norwegian. This constraint is valid!

